//
// Build-time secrets. "Secrets" in the sense every cloud platform uses it —
// both real credentials and plain configuration that varies per build. An API
// key an award programme issues, an OAuth client id that differs per edition:
// both are values a build needs and source shouldn't hard-code.
//
// Copy this file to secrets.dart (drop the .example) and fill in what you have.
// Leaving it empty is fine and is what a fresh checkout gets — every consumer
// degrades rather than failing. Some lose a feature (no spots); TOTA has no
// tower list at all, since its list itself is gated.
//
// Locally these come from `mise.local.toml`; in CI the build scripts generate
// this file from the environment. It is gitignored (see app/.gitignore).
//
// EXTENSIONS read these through `host.secret(name)`, and only the names that
// start with their own key — `sota` reads `SOTA_*`. See secret_policy.dart.
// App code reads this map directly.
//
// A flat map plus an accessor is a stopgap; a real secrets service is planned
// work, also written up in docs/extensions/README.md.

// This list is EXHAUSTIVE, and CI PARSES IT to know what to generate. Adding a
// secret means adding a line here and nothing else — every platform's build
// then picks it up from the environment. Keep the shape: one name per line, as
// `'NAME':`, commented or not. A name written any other way is silently not
// generated, which looks like a working build that quietly lost a feature.

const secrets = <String, String>{
  // The award programmes' API keys are NOT here. They ship as constants in the
  // extensions that call those endpoints (the catalog's `ham2k-wwff`,
  // `ham2k-llota` and `ham2k-tota`), because those are catalog bundles — and
  // a `.h2kext` is a zip anyone can download and read, so a value inside one
  // was never confidential whatever this store did with it. Rotating one is a
  // release, not an environment change.

  // SOTA's OAuth client id and redirect URLs are NOT here. They are public
  // identifiers of a public client (RFC 8252 — no client secret, PKCE does
  // the work), so they are plain constants in the extension that registers
  // them, the catalog's `ham2k-sota`. An override here could only misfire:
  // SOTA registers one client for every edition, so the only correct value is
  // the one in code, and an environment still carrying a per-edition id
  // authenticated as a client that does not exist in the realm.

  // Override where the realtime client (app/lib/services/push_service.dart)
  // connects — only to reach a staging or local ham2k-push Worker (api-lofi's
  // push/). Empty uses production, compiled in: both values are public
  // identifiers. The key is the Worker's PUSH_APP_KEY.
  // 'HAM2K_PUSH_HOST': '',
  // 'HAM2K_PUSH_KEY': '',

  // In-app purchases, read only by the distribution build's purchase service
  // (the public app's is a no-op, services/purchase_service.dart): a web
  // billing app's public key, and the purchase links opened in a browser.
  // Every distributed build, Dev and Next included, takes the production key
  // and links; the sandbox ones are for local runs only, since anything
  // compiled into a build can be read out of it, and a sandbox checkout takes
  // a test card. Missing any of the three, no purchases are offered.
  // 'REVENUECAT_WEB_API_KEY': '',
  // 'REVENUECAT_SUPPORTER_LINK': '',
  // 'REVENUECAT_SYNC_LINK': '',
};
